# %% [markdown]
# # Task 2: Unemployment Analysis in India (COVID-19 Impact)
# **Objective:** Perform exploratory data analysis on unemployment data to uncover regional and temporal trends, focusing on the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic in India.

# %%
# ==========================================
# 1. IMPORTS & DATA LOADING
# ==========================================
import pandas as pd
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import seaborn as sns

# Configure visual styling
sns.set_theme(style="whitegrid")
plt.rcParams["figure.figsize"] = (12, 6)

# Load the dataset (Ensure the CSV is in the same folder as this script)
file_path = "Unemployment_Rate_upto_11_2020.csv"
df = pd.read_csv(file_path)

print("Dataset Shape:", df.shape)
print("\nFirst 5 Rows:")
print(df.head())

# %%
# ==========================================
# 2. DATA CLEANING, NULL CHECK & TYPE CONVERSION
# ==========================================
# Strip leading/trailing whitespace from column names
df.columns = df.columns.str.strip()

# Rename columns for cleaner code
df.rename(columns={
    'Region': 'State',
    'Estimated Unemployment Rate (%)': 'Unemployment_Rate',
    'Estimated Employed': 'Employed',
    'Estimated Labour Participation Rate (%)': 'Labour_Participation_Rate',
    'Region.1': 'Zone'
}, inplace=True)

# Inspect missing values and data types before cleaning
print("\nMissing Values Check:\n", df.isnull().sum())
print("\nData Types Before Conversion:\n", df.dtypes)

# Drop missing values if any exist
df.dropna(inplace=True)

# Convert Date column to datetime and extract temporal features
df['Date'] = pd.to_datetime(df['Date'].str.strip(), dayfirst=True)
df['Month'] = df['Date'].dt.month_name()
df['Month_Num'] = df['Date'].dt.month
df['Year'] = df['Date'].dt.year

print("\nData Types After Conversion:\n", df.dtypes)
print("\nDescriptive Statistics:\n", df.describe())

# %% [markdown]
# ### Observation 1: Initial Data Inspection
# The dataset captures monthly unemployment metrics across Indian states from January 2020 to October 2020. After stripping whitespace from column headers and converting `Date` to datetime format, the data is clean with zero missing values.

# %%
# ==========================================
# 3. EDA: REGION-WISE & MONTH-WISE TRENDS
# ==========================================
# Region/Zone-wise average unemployment rate
zone_avg = df.groupby('Zone')['Unemployment_Rate'].mean().sort_values(ascending=False)
print("\nAverage Unemployment Rate by Zone (%):\n", zone_avg)

# Month-wise average unemployment rate across India
month_avg = df.groupby(['Month_Num', 'Month'])['Unemployment_Rate'].mean().reset_index()

plt.figure(figsize=(11, 5))
sns.lineplot(
    data=month_avg,
    x='Month',
    y='Unemployment_Rate',
    marker='o',
    linewidth=2.5,
    color='crimson'
)
plt.title("All-India Monthly Average Unemployment Rate (2020)", fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')
plt.ylabel("Unemployment Rate (%)")
plt.xlabel("Month (2020)")
plt.xticks(rotation=45)
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

# %% [markdown]
# ### Observation 2: Monthly Trend Analysis
# Unemployment stayed steady around **7%–8%** in January and February 2020 before surging dramatically to over **22%–23%** in April and May 2020 during the nationwide lockdown. From June onward, rates gradually declined toward pre-lockdown levels by October 2020.

# %%
# ==========================================
# 4. TIME-SERIES LINE CHART (MAJOR STATES)
# ==========================================
selected_states = ['Maharashtra', 'Gujarat', 'Uttar Pradesh', 'Tamil Nadu']
state_df = df[df['State'].isin(selected_states)]

plt.figure(figsize=(12, 6))
sns.lineplot(
    data=state_df,
    x='Date',
    y='Unemployment_Rate',
    hue='State',
    marker='o',
    linewidth=2
)
plt.title("Unemployment Rate Over Time Across Major Indian States (2020)", fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')
plt.ylabel("Unemployment Rate (%)")
plt.xlabel("Date")
plt.legend(title="State")
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

# %% [markdown]
# ### Observation 3: State-Level Time-Series Comparison
# While all four states experienced a spike during the April–May 2020 lockdown period, **Tamil Nadu** and **Uttar Pradesh** recorded sharper surges compared to **Gujarat** and **Maharashtra**, reflecting differences in state-level industrial closures and migrant worker movement.

# %%
# ==========================================
# 5. BAR CHART: TOP 10 STATES BY UNEMPLOYMENT
# ==========================================
top_10_states = (
    df.groupby('State')['Unemployment_Rate']
    .mean()
    .sort_values(ascending=False)
    .head(10)
    .reset_index()
)

plt.figure(figsize=(11, 6))
sns.barplot(
    data=top_10_states,
    x='Unemployment_Rate',
    y='State',
    palette='viridis',
    hue='State',
    legend=False
)
plt.title("Top 10 Indian States by Average Unemployment Rate (2020)", fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')
plt.xlabel("Average Unemployment Rate (%)")
plt.ylabel("State")
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

# %% [markdown]
# ### Observation 4: Highest Unemployment States
# **Haryana** and **Tripura** had the highest average unemployment rates across the observed timeframe (both averaging above 25%), followed by Jharkhand, Bihar, and Delhi.

# %%
# ==========================================
# 6. CORRELATION HEATMAP
# ==========================================
corr_cols = ['Unemployment_Rate', 'Employed', 'Labour_Participation_Rate']
corr_matrix = df[corr_cols].corr()

plt.figure(figsize=(7, 5))
sns.heatmap(corr_matrix, annot=True, cmap='coolwarm', fmt=".2f", linewidths=1)
plt.title("Correlation Heatmap: Unemployment, Employment & Labour Participation", fontsize=12, fontweight='bold')
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

# %% [markdown]
# ### Observation 5: Metric Correlations
# `Unemployment_Rate` has a negative correlation with `Employed` counts, reflecting job contraction during lockdowns. `Labour_Participation_Rate` shows a weak correlation with the unemployment rate because many displaced workers temporarily stopped actively searching for work during strict lockdown restrictions.

# %%
# ==========================================
# 7. PRE-COVID VS. POST-COVID COMPARISON
# ==========================================
# Split dataset at March 24, 2020 (Start of India's nationwide lockdown)
df['Period'] = np.where(
    df['Date'] < '2020-03-24',
    'Pre-COVID (Jan–Mar 2020)',
    'Post-COVID (Apr–Oct 2020)'
)

covid_comparison = (
    df.groupby('Period')[['Unemployment_Rate', 'Labour_Participation_Rate']]
    .mean()
    .reset_index()
)
print("\nPre-COVID vs. Post-COVID Summary:\n", covid_comparison)

plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))
sns.barplot(
    data=covid_comparison,
    x='Period',
    y='Unemployment_Rate',
    palette=['#2ecc71', '#e74c3c'],
    hue='Period',
    legend=False
)
plt.title("Pre-COVID vs. Post-COVID Average Unemployment Rate in India", fontsize=13, fontweight='bold')
plt.ylabel("Mean Unemployment Rate (%)")
plt.xlabel("")
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

# %% [markdown]
# ### Final Conclusion
# 1. **Lockdown Impact:** Average unemployment nearly doubled from **~7.5% Pre-COVID** to **~14.2% Post-COVID**, peaking above 23% in April–May 2020.
# 2. **Regional Disparity:** Northern and Eastern states (Haryana, Tripura, Jharkhand, Bihar) experienced the highest sustained unemployment rates.
# 3. **Economic Recovery:** Gradual unlocking from June 2020 onward steadily restored employment levels toward single digits by October 2020.
